   - Reduced NK cell proliferation and differentiation: Chronic stress can reduce the production of new NK cells and impair their maturation, leading to a lower number of functional NK cells in the body.
   - Decreased NK cell cytotoxicity: Chronic stress can reduce the ability of NK cells to kill target cells by reducing the release of cytotoxic granules (e.g., perforin and granzymes) and cytokines (e.g., perforin and granzymes).
   - Altered NK cell receptor expression: Stress hormones can alter the expression of activating and inhibitory receptors on NK cells, leading to a decrease in their ability to recognize and eliminate abnormal cells.

