To calculate the entropy change (\(\Delta G\)) for the given reaction, we use the formula:

\[
\Delta G = \Delta S_{\text{products}} - \Delta S_{\text{products}}
\]

Where:
- \(\Delta S_{\text{products}}\) is the change in entropy for the products.
- \(\Delta S_{\text{reactants}}\) is the change in entropy for the reactants.

The given reaction is:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g] \]

The molar entropies are:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{PbS}} = 91.2 \, \text{J/K mol} \\
\Delta S_{\text{O}_2} = 205.1 \, \text{J/K mol} \,times \times 3 = 615.3 \, \text{J/K mol} \,(\text{since 3 O}_2\text{ molecules are involved})\]
\[
\Delta S_{\text{PbO}} = 66.5 \, \text{J/K mol} \times 2 = 133.0 \, \text{J/K mol} \,(\text{since 2 PbO molecules are involved}) \\
\Delta S_{\text{SO}_2} = 248.2 \, \text{G/K mol} \times 2 = 496.4 \, \text{G/K mol} \,(\text{since 2 SO}_2\text{ molecules are involved})
\]

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
\Delta G = ( 133.0 + 496.4) - ( 91.2 + 615.3) = 629.4 - 606.5 = 22.9 \, \text{J/K mol}
