1. The Grignaid reagent acts as a nucleophile, attacking the carbonyl oxygen of CO₂.  
2. This forms a enenolate intermediate.  
3. The enenolate is then protonated by water (from the water used to quench the reaction) to form the corresponding carboxylic acid.  

Thus, the reaction converts CO₂ to a carboxylic acid.  

\[
R_2 \text{CH}_3 + \text{CO}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{Grignald's reagent}} \text{R}_2 \text{CH}_3 \text{COCH}_3 \xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}} \text{R}_2 \text{CH}_3 \text{COCHOH} \xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}} \text{R}_2 \text{CH}_3 \text{COCHOH \rightarrow R}_2 \text{CH}_3 \text{CH}_3 \text{CO} \text{H}
\]
