\]
Given:
\[
E^\circ(\text{Cu}^{2+}\text{/Cu}) = +0.34 \, \text{V}
\]
\[
E^\text{ox}(\text{Zn}^2\text{+/Zn}) = +0.76 \, \text{V}
\]
\[
T = 25^\circ\text{C} =290 \, \text{K}
\]

1. **Determine the standard cell potential (\(\Delta E_0\))**:
   The standard cell potential is the difference between the standard standard reduction potential of the cathode and the standard standard reduction potential of the anode.
   \[
   \Delta E_circ = E_{\text{cathode}} - E_{\text{anode}}
   \]
   In this case, the cathode is the reduction process (\(\text{Cu}^{2+}\text{+} + 2\text{e}^2 \rightarrow \text{Cu}\)) and the anode is the oxidation process (\(\text{Zn} - 2\text{e}^2 \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+}\text{}\)).
   Thus,
   \[
   \Delta E_\text{conc} = E^\circ(\text{Cu}_2\text{+/Cu}) - e^{\circ}(\text{Zn}_{2+}\text{/Zn}) = 0.34 \, \text{V} - (-0.76 \, \text{V}) = 1.00 \, \text{V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)) using Faraday’s law**:
   The relationship between \(\Delta G\) and \(\Delta E_{\text{cat}}\) is:
   \[
   \Delta G = -n F \Delta E_{\text{cat}}
   \]
   where:
   \[
   n = \text{number of electrons transferred in the reaction}
   \]
   \[
   F = Faraday’s constant = 90000 \text{C/mol}
   \]
   For the given reaction, \(n = 2\) electrons are transferred.
   \[
   \Delta G = -2 \times 90000 \, \text{C/mol} \times 1.00 \, \text{V} = -190000 \, \text{kJ/mol} = -19000 \, \text{kJ/mol}
   \]

Thus, the Gibbs free energy change for the electrochemical reaction is \(\boxed{-19000 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
