For each component, the probability of failure \( P_i(t) \) is given by \( P_I(t) = \lambda t \).

**a) Series System**
In a series system, the system fails if any component fails. The reliability of the system is the sum of the probabilities that all components are working at time \( t \).

\[
R_S(t) = S_1(t) S_2(t) S_3(t) \implies \sum_{i} \quad S_i(t)
\]

Since \( S_I(t) = 1 - \lambda t \), the reliability of the system is:

\[
R_S(t) = \sum_{i} (1 - \lambda t) = (1 - \lambda t) (1 - \lambda t) (1 - \lambda t) = [1 - \lambda t)^n \quad \text{(for 8 components)}
\]

Thus, for a series system with \( n \) independent components, the reliability is:

\[
R_S(t) = [1 - \lambda t)^n
\]

