- High-energy compounds are molecules that store energy in their chemical bonds, which can be released to drive biochemical reactions.  
- The standard free energy change (\(\Delta G_{s'}\)) is a measure of the energy released when a compound is hydrolyzed under standard conditions.  
- ATP (adenosine triphosphate) has a \(\Delta G_{s'}\) of approximately \(\boxed{-38 \text{ kJ/mol}}\).  
- Other high-energy compounds include:  
  - ATP-CoA: \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -29 \text{ kJ/mol}\)  
  - Glucose-3-phosphate: \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -25 \text{ kJ//- mol}\)  
  - Glucose-6-phosphate: \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -24 \text{ kJ/mol}\)  
  - Inorganic phosphate (Pi): \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -22 \text{ kJ/mol}\)  
  - Fumarate to succinyl CoA: \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -28 \text{ kJ/mol}\)  
  - NADP\(^+\) to NADP\(^+\): \(\Delta G_{s'} \approx -24 \text{ kJ/mol}\)  
- ATP has the largest \(\Delta G' \). This is why it is used as the primary energy currency in cells.  

