"A sphere of mass \( m \) and radius \( R \) is rolling without slipping on a horizontal surface with an initial velocity \( v_0 \). If the sphere is subject to a constant acceleration \( a \), what is the velocity of the sphere after a time \( t \)?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
A sphere of mass \( m \) and radius \( R \) is rolling without slipping on a horizontal surface with an initial velocity \( v_0 \). If the sphere is subject to a constant acceleration \( a \), what is the velocity of the sphere after a time \( t \)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the velocity of the sphere after time \( t\), we use the principle of angular motion and linear motion. Since the sphere rolls without slipping, the angular and linear components are related by \( v = \omega r \), where \( v \) is the linear velocity, \( \omega \) is the angular velocity, and \( r \) is the radius. In this case, \( r = R \), so \( v = \omega R \).

1. **Linear motion**: The linear velocity \( v \) is given by the initial velocity \( v_0 \) plus the acceleration \( a \) times the time \( t \):
   \[
   v = v_0 + at \quad \text{(1)}
   \]

2. **Angular motion**: The angular velocity \( \omega \) is given by the initial angular velocity \( \omega_0 \) plus the angular acceleration \( \alpha \) times the time \( t \):
   \[
   \omega = \omega_0 + \alpha t \quad \text{(2)}
   \]
   Since the sphere starts rolling with a constant acceleration \( a \), the angular acceleration is given by \( \omega = \omega_0 + \frac{a}{R} t \).
   Since the sphere starts with a constant initial velocity \( v_0 \), the initial angular velocity is \( \omega_0 = \frac{v_0}{R} \). Thus,
   \[
   \omega = \frac{v_0}{R} + \frac{a}{R} t \quad \text{(3)}
   \]

3. **Rolling without slip**: The relationship between linear and angular motion is \( v = \omega R \), so:
   \[
