What is the amount of heat released when 2.5 moles of propane (C3H8) is completely burned in oxygen gas (O2) according to the balanced equation: 

C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O 

given that the standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The balanced equation is:
\[ C_3H_8 + 5O_2 \rightarrow 3CO_2 + 4H_2O \]
The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is given as \(\Delta H = -2220 \, \text{kJ/mol}\). This means that 1 mole of propane releases 2220 kJ of heat when completely burned.

Given that we have 2.5 moles of propane, the total heat released is:
\[ \text{Heat released} = 2.5 \, \text{mol} \times 2220 \, \text{kJ/mol} = 5550 \, \text{kJ} \]

Thus, the amount of heat released is \(\boxed{5550 \, \text{kJ}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

