","To find the number of roots of the equation \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\), we can compare the two functions \(S(x) = \sin x\) and \(F(x) = \frac{x}{20}\).

The equation \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\) has the same number of roots as the intersection of the two lines \(\sin x\) and \(\frac{x}{20}\).
The range of \(\sin x\) is \([-1, 1)\). Thus, the roots of \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\) must satisfy \[-1 \geq \frac{x}{20} \geq 1\), which means \[-20 \geq x \geq 20\).
However, the range of \(\sin x\) is \[-1, 1\), so the roots must satisfy \[-1 \geq \frac{x}{20}\), which means \(x \leq -20\), and \(\frac{x}{20} \geq 1\), which means \(x \geq 20\).
Thus, the roots must lie in the interval \[-20, 20\).

The intersection of \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\) is the same as the intersection of the line \(y = \frac{x}{20}\) and the curve \(y = \sin x\).
The roots are the solutions to the equation \(\sin x - \frac{x}{20} = 0\).
The intersection of \(\sin x\) and \(\frac{x}{20}\) is:
\begin{-
- \begin{aligned}
\text{For } x = 0, \sin 0 = 0 \text{ and } \frac{0}{20} = 0, \text{ so }x = 0 \text{ is a root.}
\end{aligned}
- \begin{aligned}
\text{For } x < 0, \sin x < 0 \text{ and } \frac{x}{20} < 0, \text{ so the two functions are both negative.}
\end{aligned}
- \begin{aligned}
\text{For } x > 0, \sin x > 0 \text{ and } \frac{x}{20} > 0, \text{ so the two functions are both positive.}
\endaligned}
- \begin{aligned}
\text{For } x \geq 20, \sin x \in -1, 1 \text{ and } \frac{x}{20} \geq 1, \text{ so the equation has no solutions.}
\end{aligned}
