</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law: \(a = \frac{F}{m}\), where \(F\) is the force acting on the proton and \(m$ is its mass. The force on the proton is given by Coulomb's law: \(F = q E\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. Thus, the acceleration is:
\[
a = \frac{q E}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19 \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.66 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.5 \times 10^0 \, \text{m/s}^2 = 4.5 \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial velocity \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). The proton's final velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the kinematic equation \(v = v_0 + at\), we can find the time \(t\):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.5 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7,838.8 \, \text{s}.
\]

Thus, the time is approximately \(7,838 \, \text{s}\).

