Calculate the change in Gibbs free energy when 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas at a constant temperature of 298 K and a pressure of 1 bar, given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water to be -237.1 kJ/mol.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we use the Gibbs free energy change equation:  
\[ \Delta G = n \Delta G_{0} \]  
where \( \Delta G \) is the Gibbs free energy change for the given amount of reactants, \( n \) is the number of moles of the product, and \( \Delta G_0 \) is the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of the product.  
The reaction is:  
\[ 2 \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O} \]  
Given:  
- Standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water (\(\Delta G_0_{\text{H}_2\text{O}}\)) = -237.1 kJ/mol  
- Number of moles of hydrogen gas (\( n \)) = 2 mol  
- Number of moles of oxygen gas (\( n \)) = 1 mol  
The stoichiometric ratio shows that 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas to produce 2 moles of water.  
Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy is:  
\[ \Delta G = 2 \times \Delta G_0_{\text{H}_2\text{O}} = 2 \times (-237.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}) = -464.2 \, \text{kJ} \]  
The change in Gibbs free energy is \(\boxed{-464.2 \, \text{kJ}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

