","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Distance} \times \sin(\theta)
\]  
where:  
- Force is the force applied (in N).  
- Distance is the distance moved (in m).  
- \(\theta\) is the angle between the force and the displacement.  

Given:  
- Force = 60.0 N  
- Distance = 10.0 m  
- Angle = 66.0°  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times \sin(66.0°)
\]  
\[
\text{Work} = 600 \text{ N m} \times \sin(66.0°)
\]  
\[
\text{Work} = 600 \text{ N m} \times 0.893
\]  
\[
