<USER_REQUEST>
A particle of mass 30 kg is subjected to a force of 32 N. Calculate the acceleration of the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the acceleration of the particle, we use Newton's second law of motion, which states that the net force acting on an object is equal to its mass times its acceleration:  
\[
\text{F} = m \cdot a
\]  
Where:  
- \( F \) is the force in New-seconds (N)  
- \( m \) is the mass in kilograms (kg)  
- \( a \) is the acceleration in meters-second\(^{-2}\) (m/s²)  
Given:  
- Mass \( m = 30 \, \text{kg} \)  
- Force \( F = 32 \, \text{N} \)  
Solving for acceleration \( a \):  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{32 \, \text{N}}{30 \, \text{kg}} = \frac{32}{30} \, \text{m/s}^2 = \frac{18.6}{15} \, \text{m/s}^2 \approx 1.27 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the acceleration of the particle is \(\boxed{1.27 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

