Given:  
- Mass \( m = 1 \, \text{kg} \).  
- Radius \( L = 0.5 \, \text{m} \).  
- Tangential acceleration \( a = 2 \, \text{m/s}^2 \).  
- The pulley is a disc, so its moment of inertia is \( I = \frac{m}{I_2} \times L^2 = \frac{1}{2} m l^2 \).  
- The force \( F \) is applied at the rim of the pulley, so \( \alpha_0 = L = 0.5 \, \text{ m} \).  
The angular acceleration \( \alpha \) is related to the tangential acceleration \( a \) by \( a = L \alpha \), so \( \alpha = \frac{a}{L} = \frac{2}{0.5} = 4 \, \text{rad/s}^2 \).  
The torque \( \tau \) acting on the pulley is given by \( \tau = I \alpha \), where \( I \) is the moment of inertia of the pulley. For a disk, \( I = \frac{1}{2} m l^2 = \frac{1}{2} ( 1 \, \text{kg}) ( 0.5 \, \text{m} ) = 0.25 \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^2 \).  
Thus, the torque is \( \tau = 0.25 \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^2 \times 4 \, \text{rad/s}^2 = 1 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} \).  
