","The coefficient of restitution \( e \) is defined as the ratio of the relative velocities of the two bodies after the collision to the relative velocities before the collision. In this case, the ball and the wall are the two bodies. The velocity of the wall is constant and zero, so the relative velocities are the velocities of the ball before and after the collision. Thus,
\[ e = \frac{v_2}{v_1} = \frac{v_2}{v_0} = \frac{1}{3} \]
where \( v_0 \) is the initial velocity of the ball and \( v_2 \) is the final velocity of the ball after the collision.

The kinetic energy of the ball before the collision is \( KE_1 = \frac{1}{2} m v_0 \mid \), and the kinetic energy after the collision is \( KE_2 = \frac{11}{2} m v_2 \mid \).
The fraction of kinetic energy lost is
\[ \frac{\Delta KE}{KE_1} = \frac{KE_1 - KE_2}{KE_1} = \frac{1 - \frac{KE_2}{KE_1}}{1} =frac{1 - \frac{\frac{1}{2} m v_2 \mid}{\frac{1}{2} m v_0 \mid}}{1} =frac{1 - \frac{|v_2|}{|v_0|}} = 1 - e = 1 - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3}. \]

Thus, the fraction of kinetic energy lost is \(\frac{2}{3}\).

The coefficient of restitution is given as \( e = \frac{1}{3} \).
The initial kinetic energy is \( KE_1 = \frac{1}{2} m v_0 \mid \).
The final kinetic energy is \( KE_2 = \frac{1}{2} m v_2 \mid \).
Since \( e = \frac{v_2}{v_0} = \frac{1}{3} \), we have \( v_2 = \frac{1}{3} v_0 \).
Thus, \( KE_2 = \frac{1}{2} m \leftleft(\frac{1}{3} v_0\right| = \frac{1}{3} \left(\frac{1}{2} m v_0 \mid = \frac{1}{3} KE_1 \).
The change in kinetic energy is \( \Delta KE = KE_1 - KE_2 = KE_1 - \frac{1}{3} KE_1 = \frac{2}{3} KE_1 \).
The fraction of kinetic energy lost is
\[ \frac{\Delta KE}{KE_1} = \frac{\frac{2}{3} KE_1}{KE_1} = \frac{2}{3}. \]

